## Proof

The statement is **false** in general. We provide an explicit counterexample.

**Setup.** Let $A = M_2(\mathbb{C})$, the C\*-algebra of $2\times 2$ complex matrices, with the usual involution $a^* = \bar{a}^T$ and $|a| = \sqrt{a^*a}$.

**The positive operator.** Define $T: A \to A$ by $T(a) = a^T$ (matrix transpose). This is a $\mathbb{C}$-linear map, hence $T \in B(A)$.

*Claim:* $T$ is positive, i.e., $T(A^+) \subseteq A^+$.

*Proof of claim.* An element $a \in M_2(\mathbb{C})$ is positive iff $a = a^*$ and all eigenvalues of $a$ are $\geq 0$. If $a = a^*$, then $a_{ij} = \bar{a}_{ji}$, so $\bar{a}_{ij} = a_{ji}$, which gives $(a^T)^* = \bar{a} = a^T$; thus $a^T$ is self-adjoint. Since $a^T$ has the same characteristic polynomial as $a$ (as $\det(a^T - \lambda I) = \det((a-\lambda I)^T) = \det(a - \lambda I)$), $a^T$ has the same eigenvalues as $a$, hence all $\geq 0$. Therefore $a^T \in A^+$. $\checkmark$

**The counterexample.** Take
$$f = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \in A.$$

*Compute $T|f|$:* We have $f^* = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$, so
$$f^*f = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad |f| = \sqrt{f^*f} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.$$
Since $|f|$ is diagonal, $T|f| = |f|^T = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$.

*Compute $|Tf|$:* We have $Tf = f^T = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$, so $(Tf)^* = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$, and
$$(Tf)^*(Tf) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad |Tf| = \sqrt{(Tf)^*(Tf)} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.$$

**Conclusion.**
$$|Tf| = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \neq \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = T|f|.$$

Thus $|Tf| \neq T|f|$ for this positive operator $T$ and element $f$. The identity $|Tf| = T|f|$ does **not** hold for positive operators in general. $\blacksquare$

